{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c435","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0c3a4-a344-7f92-9989-00a35b123d9d"},"sequence":117,"recorded_at":1780531400000229,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"60c840df-7c16-4e67-9414-5d947d88b14a","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"command_id":"60c840df-7c16-4e67-9414-5d947d88b14a","event":{"chunk":"Read text file `/mnt/c/Users/MDP/collatz-analysis/Collatz/research_track/CP25_SIGN_SENSITIVE_CYCLE_PILOT/CP25_REPORT.md`.\n1|# CP25 — Sign-Sensitive Cycle-Word Pilot\n2|\n3|**Date:** 2026-09-20  \n4|**Status:** KILL (planned sign + integrality + realizability route)  \n5|**Collatz status:** OPEN / NOT PROVED\n6|\n7|## 1. Question\n8|\n9|For\n10|\n11|\\[\n12|H_{p,b}(x)=\\begin{cases}\n13|x/2,&x\\equiv0\\pmod2,\\\\\n14|(px+b)/2,&x\\equiv1\\pmod2,\n15|\\end{cases}\n16|\\qquad p\\ge3\\text{ odd},\\ b\\in\\{-1,+1\\},\n17|\\]\n18|\n19|can the sign of the cycle denominator, its divisibility, and separate parity-word realizability supply a nontrivial sign-sensitive obstruction that survives the CP23/CP24 energy kill?\n20|\n21|## 2. Definitions\n22|\n23|Let `w=(eps_0,...,eps_{m-1})` be a nonempty binary word with `eps_0=1`, let\n24|\n25|\\[\n26|k=\\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}\\varepsilon_j,\n27|\\qquad\n28|B_w=\\sum_{j:\\varepsilon_j=1}2^j p^{\\sum_{i>j}\\varepsilon_i},\n29|\\qquad\n30|D=2^m-p^k.\n31|\\]\n32|\n33|Formal composition along `w` gives\n34|\n35|\\[\n36|H_w(x)=\\frac{p^k x+bB_w}{2^m}.\n37|\\]\n38|\n39|Since `m>0`, `k>0`, and `p` is odd, `D` cannot vanish. A formal cycle candidate is\n40|\n41|\\[\n42|x=\\frac{bB_w}{D}.\n43|\\]\n44|\n45|This is the standard parity-vector/cycle setup rather than a new representation: the classical literature assigns each length-`m` vector a unique residue class, and Böhm–Sontacchi already use the finite enumeration of binary vectors to decide fixed cycle lengths.[1][2][3]\n46|\n47|## 3. Exact lemma: integrality already forces realizability\n48|\n49|### Lemma\n50|\n51|If `D` divides `b B_w`, then the integer `x=bB_w/D` follows the parity word `w` under the actual piecewise map `H_{p,b}` and returns to `x` after `m` steps. This remains true when `x<0`.\n52|\n53|### Proof\n54|\n55|For the prefix `w_{<j}` let `k_j` be its number of ones and `B_j` its affine numerator. Write\n56|\n57|\\[\n58|N_j=p^{k_j}x+bB_j.\n59|\\]\n60|\n61|Splitting the full numerator into prefix and suffix gives, for an integer suffix numerator `C_j`,\n62|\n63|\\[\n64|p^kx+bB_w=p^{k-k_j}N_j+b2^jC_j.\n65|\\]\n66|\n67|The cycle equation is\n68|\n69|\\[\n70|p^kx+bB_w=2^m x.\n71|\\]\n72|\n73|Reducing the split identity modulo `2^j` and using that `p` is odd shows `2^j | N_j`. Hence\n74|\n75|\\[\n76|y_j=N_j/2^j\n77|\\]\n78|\n79|is an integer for every prefix.\n80|\n81|If `eps_j=0`, divisibility of `N_{j+1}=N_j` by `2^{j+1}` makes `y_j` even. If `eps_j=1`, divisibility of\n82|\n83|\\[\n84|N_{j+1}=pN_j+b2^j\n85|\\]\n86|\n87|by `2^{j+1}` makes `p y_j+b` even; since both `p` and `b` are odd, `y_j` is odd. Thus every formal branch is the branch selected by the actual parity of the current integer. Finally, `N_m/2^m=x`, so the actual trajectory returns after `m` steps. The argument is over the integers and does not assume `x>0`. ∎\n88|\n89|### Corollaries\n90|\n91|1. `D | bB_w` is equivalent to `D | B_w`; divisibility is independent of the sign `b`.\n92|2. `B_w>0`, so `x>0` exactly when `sign(D)=b`.\n93|3. Parity-word realizability is not a second filter after divisibility. It is automatic.\n94|4. For `p=3`, the same divisible words are split by the denominator sign: the `+1` map keeps `D>0`, while the `-1` map keeps `D<0`.\n95|\n96|## 4. Exhaustive finite computation\n97|\n98|`cycle_word_sign_pilot.py` enumerates every word beginning with `1`, computes exact integer `B_w`, `D`, and `x`, verifies the actual trajectory, and canonicalizes primitive positive cycles. `results_m22.json` covers\n99|\n100|\\[\n101|\\sum_{m=1}^{22}2^{m-1}=4,194,303\n102|\\]\n103|\n104|words for each map, or 12,582,909 map/word rows in total.\n105|\n106|| Map | Divisible rows | Positive rows | Realized positive rows | Distinct primitive positive cycles |\n107||---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n108|| `3n+1` | 61 | 11 | 11 | 1 |\n109|| `3n-1` | 61 | 50 | 50 | 3 |\n110|| `5n+1` | 37 | 26 | 26 | 3 |\n111|\n112|The positive `3n-1` primitive cycles found are:\n113|\n114|- `(1)`;\n115|- `(5,7,10)`;\n116|- `(17,25,37,55,82,41,61,91,136,68,34)`.\n117|\n118|Under sign reflection these agree with the classical negative `3n+1` cycles based at `-1`, `-5`, and `-17`, whose periods are reported as 1, 3, and 11.[3]\n119|\n120|The counts are **word rows**, so repetitions of a primitive cycle at lengths `2m`, `3m`, and so on are counted again. They are not counts of distinct cycles.\n121|\n122|## 5. Verdict\n123|\n124|**KILL** for the planned CP25 route.\n125|\n126|The sign is genuinely visible, but only through the classical positivity condition\n127|\n128|\\[\n129|2^m>3^k\\quad(b=+1),\n130|\\qquad\n131|2^m<3^k\\quad(b=-1).\n132|\\]\n133|\n134|Divisibility does not see `b`, and realizability contributes no additional constraint once divisibility holds. Therefore the proposed combination does not advance beyond the classical parity-vector cycle equation. The exact scan is a regression check and a finite census, not a proof of the Collatz conjecture or of global cycle exclusion.\n135|\n136|## 6. Safe redirect\n137|\n138|Do not extend the same scan to larger `m` and call that theoretical progress. A next lemma must use information absent from the scalar equation `D x=bB_w`.\n139|\n140|Candidate pilot:\n141|\n142|> **Rotation-gap divisibility.** For cyclic rotations `rho^i(w)`, derive exact differences `B_{rho^i(w)}-B_{rho^j(w)}` and test whether `D` dividing every rotation numerator forces forbidden local word structure in the `D>0` regime.\n143|\n144|Fast kill conditions:\n145|\n146|1. the claimed restriction reduces to `D|B_w` or `D>0`;\n147|2. it also falsely excludes one of the known `3n-1` cycles above;\n148|3. exhaustive word tests find a nontrivial `D>0` counterexample;\n149|4. the argument is sign-blind and supplies no condition beyond an already-classical circuit class.\n150|\n151|A literature/novelty check must precede any `[PROOF]` label.\n152|\n153|## 7. Reproduction\n154|\n155|```bash\n156|python -B -m unittest research_track/CP25_SIGN_SENSITIVE_CYCLE_PILOT/test_cycle_word_sign_pilot.py\n157|python -B research_track/CP25_SIGN_SENSITIVE_CYCLE_PILOT/cycle_word_sign_pilot.py \\\n158|  --max-m 22 \\\n159|  --output research_track/CP25_SIGN_SENSITIVE_CYCLE_PILOT/results_m22.json\n160|```\n161|\n162|## Sources\n163|\n164|[1] https://eudml.org/doc/290184 — Böhm and Sontacchi (1978): cycles of given length\n165|    > \"Böhm, Corrado, and Sontacchi, Giovanna. \"On the existence of cycles of given length in integer sequences like $x_{n+1} = x_{n}/2$ if $x_{n}$ even, and $x_{n+1} = 3 x_{n} + 1$.\"\"\n166|[2] https://www.cecm.sfu.ca/organics/papers/lagarias/paper/html/node4-an.shtml — Lagarias survey: parity vectors and residue classes\n167|    > \"Theorem B allows one to associate with each vector of length k a unique congruence class.\"\n168|[3] https://www.cecm.sfu.ca/organics/papers/lagarias/paper/html/node8-an.shtml — Lagarias survey: non-trivial cycles\n169|    > \"Böhm and Sontacchi also noted that this gives an (inefficient) finite procedure for deciding if there are any cycles of a given length k.\"","final_result":true,"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0c3a5-1f7a-76f1-b742-9f2ae6599b1f"},"kind":"task_lifecycle","run_stream":{"id":"60c840df-7c16-4e67-9414-5d947d88b14a","kind":"run"},"task_id":"01a0c3a5-1f7a-76f1-b742-9f2ae6599b1f","task_stream":{"id":"01a0c3a5-1f7a-76f1-b742-9f2ae6599b1f","kind":"task"}}}
