{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c439","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0c3a4-a344-7f92-9989-00a35b123d9d"},"sequence":119,"recorded_at":1780531400000233,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"60c840df-7c16-4e67-9414-5d947d88b14a","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"call_id":"call_01a0c3a5256677b6988200b3a34fbcbe","command_id":"60c840df-7c16-4e67-9414-5d947d88b14a","correlation_facts":{"outcome":"success","tool_name":"read_file"},"kind":"tool_result","run_stream":{"id":"60c840df-7c16-4e67-9414-5d947d88b14a","kind":"run"},"text":"Read text file `/mnt/c/Users/MDP/collatz-analysis/Collatz/research_track/CP25_SIGN_SENSITIVE_CYCLE_PILOT/CP25_REPORT.md`.\n1|# CP25 — Sign-Sensitive Cycle-Word Pilot\n2|\n3|**Date:** 2026-09-20  \n4|**Status:** KILL (planned sign + integrality + realizability route)  \n5|**Collatz status:** OPEN / NOT PROVED\n6|\n7|## 1. Question\n8|\n9|For\n10|\n11|\\[\n12|H_{p,b}(x)=\\begin{cases}\n13|x/2,&x\\equiv0\\pmod2,\\\\\n14|(px+b)/2,&x\\equiv1\\pmod2,\n15|\\end{cases}\n16|\\qquad p\\ge3\\text{ odd},\\ b\\in\\{-1,+1\\},\n17|\\]\n18|\n19|can the sign of the cycle denominator, its divisibility, and separate parity-word realizability supply a nontrivial sign-sensitive obstruction that survives the CP23/CP24 energy kill?\n20|\n21|## 2. Definitions\n22|\n23|Let `w=(eps_0,...,eps_{m-1})` be a nonempty binary word with `eps_0=1`, let\n24|\n25|\\[\n26|k=\\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}\\varepsilon_j,\n27|\\qquad\n28|B_w=\\sum_{j:\\varepsilon_j=1}2^j p^{\\sum_{i>j}\\varepsilon_i},\n29|\\qquad\n30|D=2^m-p^k.\n31|\\]\n32|\n33|Formal composition along `w` gives\n34|\n35|\\[\n36|H_w(x)=\\frac{p^k x+bB_w}{2^m}.\n37|\\]\n38|\n39|Since `m>0`, `k>0`, and `p` is odd, `D` cannot vanish. A formal cycle candidate is\n40|\n41|\\[\n42|x=\\frac{bB_w}{D}.\n43|\\]\n44|\n45|This is the standard parity-vector/cycle setup rather than a new representation: the classical literature assigns each length-`m` vector a unique residue class, and Böhm–Sontacchi already use the finite enumeration of binary vectors to decide fixed cycle lengths.[1][2][3]\n46|\n47|## 3. Exact lemma: integrality already forces realizability\n48|\n49|### Lemma\n50|\n51|If `D` divides `b B_w`, then the integer `x=bB_w/D` follows the parity word `w` under the actual piecewise map `H_{p,b}` and returns to `x` after `m` steps. This remains true when `x<0`.\n52|\n53|### Proof\n54|\n55|For the prefix `w_{<j}` let `k_j` be its number of ones and `B_j` its affine numerator. Write\n56|\n57|\\[\n58|N_j=p^{k_j}x+bB_j.\n59|\\]\n60|\n61|Splitting the full numerator into prefix and suffix gives, for an integer suffix numerator `C_j`,\n62|\n63|\\[\n64|p^kx+bB_w=p^{k-k_j}N_j+b2^jC_j.\n65|\\]\n66|\n67|The cycle equation is\n68|\n69|\\[\n70|p^kx+bB_w=2^m x.\n71|\\]\n72|\n73|Reducing the split identity modulo `2^j` and using that `p` is odd shows `2^j | N_j`. Hence\n74|\n75|\\[\n76|y_j=N_j/2^j\n77|\\]\n78|\n79|is an integer for every prefix.\n80|\n81|If `eps_j=0`, divisibility of `N_{j+1}=N_j` by `2^{j+1}` makes `y_j` even. If `eps_j=1`, divisibility of\n82|\n83|\\[\n84|N_{j+1}=pN_j+b2^j\n85|\\]\n86|\n87|by `2^{j+1}` makes `p y_j+b` even; since both `p` and `b` are odd, `y_j` is odd. Thus every formal branch is the branch selected by the actual parity of the current integer. Finally, `N_m/2^m=x`, so the actual trajectory returns after `m` steps. The argument is over the integers and does not assume `x>0`. ∎\n88|\n89|### Corollaries\n90|\n91|1. `D | bB_w` is equivalent to `D | B_w`; divisibility is independent of the sign `b`.\n92|2. `B_w>0`, so `x>0` exactly when `sign(D)=b`.\n93|3. Parity-word realizability is not a second filter after divisibility. It is automatic.\n94|4. For `p=3`, the same divisible words are split by the denominator sign: the `+1` map keeps `D>0`, while the `-1` map keeps `D<0`.\n95|\n96|## 4. Exhaustive finite computation\n97|\n98|`cycle_word_sign_pilot.py` enumerates every word beginning with `1`, computes exact integer `B_w`, `D`, and `x`, verifies the actual trajectory, and canonicalizes primitive positive cycles. `results_m22.json` covers\n99|\n100|\\[\n101|\\sum_{m=1}^{22}2^{m-1}=4,194,303\n102|\\]\n103|\n104|words for each map, or 12,582,909 map/word rows in total.\n105|\n106|| Map | Divisible rows | Positive rows | Realized positive rows | Distinct primitive positive cycles |\n107||---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n108|| `3n+1` | 61 | 11 | 11 | 1 |\n109|| `3n-1` | 61 | 50 | 50 | 3 |\n110|| `5n+1` | 37 | 26 | 26 | 3 |\n111|\n112|The positive `3n-1` primitive cycles found are:\n113|\n114|- `(1)`;\n115|- `(5,7,10)`;\n116|- `(17,25,37,55,82,41,61,91,136,68,34)`.\n117|\n118|Under sign reflection these agree with the classical negative `3n+1` cycles based at `-1`, `-5`, and `-17`, whose periods are reported as 1, 3, and 11.[3]\n119|\n120|The counts are **word rows**, so repetitions of a primitive cycle at lengths `2m`, `3m`, and so on are counted again. They are not counts of distinct cycles.\n121|\n122|## 5. Verdict\n123|\n124|**KILL** for the planned CP25 route.\n125|\n126|The sign is genuinely visible, but only through the classical positivity condition\n127|\n128|\\[\n129|2^m>3^k\\quad(b=+1),\n130|\\qquad\n131|2^m<3^k\\quad(b=-1).\n132|\\]\n133|\n134|Divisibility does not see `b`, and realizability contributes no additional constraint once divisibility holds. Therefore the proposed combination does not advance beyond the classical parity-vector cycle equation. The exact scan is a regression check and a finite census, not a proof of the Collatz conjecture or of global cycle exclusion.\n135|\n136|## 6. Safe redirect\n137|\n138|Do not extend the same scan to larger `m` and call that theoretical progress. A next lemma must use information absent from the scalar equation `D x=bB_w`.\n139|\n140|Candidate pilot:\n141|\n142|> **Rotation-gap divisibility.** For cyclic rotations `rho^i(w)`, derive exact differences `B_{rho^i(w)}-B_{rho^j(w)}` and test whether `D` dividing every rotation numerator forces forbidden local word structure in the `D>0` regime.\n143|\n144|Fast kill conditions:\n145|\n146|1. the claimed restriction reduces to `D|B_w` or `D>0`;\n147|2. it also falsely excludes one of the known `3n-1` cycles above;\n148|3. exhaustive word tests find a nontrivial `D>0` counterexample;\n149|4. the argument is sign-blind and supplies no condition beyond an already-classical circuit class.\n150|\n151|A literature/novelty check must precede any `[PROOF]` label.\n152|\n153|## 7. Reproduction\n154|\n155|```bash\n156|python -B -m unittest research_track/CP25_SIGN_SENSITIVE_CYCLE_PILOT/test_cycle_word_sign_pilot.py\n157|python -B research_track/CP25_SIGN_SENSITIVE_CYCLE_PILOT/cycle_word_sign_pilot.py \\\n158|  --max-m 22 \\\n159|  --output research_track/CP25_SIGN_SENSITIVE_CYCLE_PILOT/results_m22.json\n160|```\n161|\n162|## Sources\n163|\n164|[1] https://eudml.org/doc/290184 — Böhm and Sontacchi (1978): cycles of given length\n165|    > \"Böhm, Corrado, and Sontacchi, Giovanna. \"On the existence of cycles of given length in integer sequences like $x_{n+1} = x_{n}/2$ if $x_{n}$ even, and $x_{n+1} = 3 x_{n} + 1$.\"\"\n166|[2] https://www.cecm.sfu.ca/organics/papers/lagarias/paper/html/node4-an.shtml — Lagarias survey: parity vectors and residue classes\n167|    > \"Theorem B allows one to associate with each vector of length k a unique congruence class.\"\n168|[3] https://www.cecm.sfu.ca/organics/papers/lagarias/paper/html/node8-an.shtml — Lagarias survey: non-trivial cycles\n169|    > \"Böhm and Sontacchi also noted that this gives an (inefficient) finite procedure for deciding if there are any cycles of a given length k.\""}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c43b","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0c3a4-a344-7f92-9989-00a35b123d9d"},"sequence":120,"recorded_at":1780531400000235,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"60c840df-7c16-4e67-9414-5d947d88b14a","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"call_id":"call_01a0c3a5274f76fe86dafb7029886560","command_id":"60c840df-7c16-4e67-9414-5d947d88b14a","correlation_facts":{"outcome":"success","tool_name":"read_file"},"kind":"tool_result","run_stream":{"id":"60c840df-7c16-4e67-9414-5d947d88b14a","kind":"run"},"text":"Read text file `/mnt/c/Users/MDP/collatz-analysis/Collatz/research_track/CP25_SIGN_SENSITIVE_CYCLE_PILOT/lift_coherence.py`.\n1|#!/usr/bin/env python3\n2|\"\"\"Exact oriented lift-coherence diagnostics for CP25.\"\"\"\n3|from __future__ import annotations\n4|\n5|from fractions import Fraction\n6|from typing import Iterable\n7|\n8|\n9|def _bits(word: Iterable[int]) -> tuple[int, ...]:\n10|    bits = tuple(word)\n11|    if not bits or any(bit not in (0, 1) for bit in bits):\n12|        raise ValueError(\"word must be a nonempty binary iterable\")\n13|    return bits\n14|\n15|\n16|def B_value(p: int, word: Iterable[int]) -> int:\n17|    bits = _bits(word)\n18|    if p < 3 or p % 2 == 0:\n19|        raise ValueError(\"p must be an odd integer >= 3\")\n20|    value = 0\n21|    for j, bit in enumerate(bits):\n22|        if bit:\n23|            value = p * value + (1 << j)\n24|    return value\n25|\n26|\n27|def h_residue(p: int, b: int, word: Iterable[int]) -> int:\n28|    bits = _bits(word)\n29|    if b not in (-1, 1):\n30|        raise ValueError(\"b must be +1 or -1\")\n31|    modulus = 1 << len(bits)\n32|    pk = pow(p, sum(bits), modulus)\n33|    residue = (-b * B_value(p, bits) * pow(pk, -1, modulus)) % modulus\n34|    return residue or modulus\n35|\n36|\n37|def is_primitive(word: Iterable[int]) -> bool:\n38|    bits = _bits(word)\n39|    m = len(bits)\n40|    return all(m % d or bits != bits[:d] * (m // d) for d in range(1, m))\n41|\n42|\n43|def rotations(word: Iterable[int]) -> tuple[tuple[int, ...], ...]:\n44|    bits = _bits(word)\n45|    return tuple(bits[i:] + bits[:i] for i in range(len(bits)))\n46|\n47|\n48|def lift_profile(p: int, word: Iterable[int]) -> dict[str, object]:\n49|    bits = _bits(word)\n50|    m = len(bits)\n51|    k = sum(bits)\n52|    if k == 0:\n53|        raise ValueError(\"word must contain at least one odd step\")\n54|    D = (1 << m) - p**k\n55|    b = 1 if D > 0 else -1\n56|    A = abs(D)\n57|    rows = []\n58|    modulus = 1 << m\n59|    for i, rotated in enumerate(rotations(bits)):\n60|        B = B_value(p, rotated)\n61|        q = Fraction(B, A)\n62|        h = h_residue(p, b, rotated)\n63|        t_numerator = B - A * h\n64|        if t_numerator % modulus:\n65|            raise RuntimeError(\"lift residue failed to make B-Ah divisible by 2^m\")\n66|        t = t_numerator // modulus\n67|        if (q >= h) != (t >= 0):\n68|            raise RuntimeError(\"lift dominance and carry sign disagree\")\n69|        rows.append(\n70|            {\n71|                \"index\": i,\n72|                \"word\": rotated,\n73|                \"B\": B,\n74|                \"q\": q,\n75|                \"h\": h,\n76|                \"t\": t,\n77|                \"dominates\": t >= 0,\n78|            }\n79|        )\n80|    for i, row in enumerate(rows):\n81|        next_h = rows[(i + 1) % m][\"h\"]\n82|        epsilon = row[\"word\"][0]\n83|        carry_numerator = (p**epsilon) * row[\"h\"] + b * epsilon - 2 * next_h\n84|        if carry_numerator % modulus:\n85|            raise RuntimeError(\"adjacent lift representatives do not define an integer carry\")\n86|        row[\"carry\"] = carry_numerator // modulus\n87|    return {\n88|        \"p\": p,\n89|        \"word\": bits,\n90|        \"m\": m,\n91|        \"k\": k,\n92|        \"D\": D,\n93|        \"b\": b,\n94|        \"A\": A,\n95|        \"primitive\": is_primitive(bits),\n96|        \"integral\": B_value(p, bits) % A == 0,\n97|        \"all_dominate\": all(row[\"dominates\"] for row in rows),\n98|        \"rotations\": tuple(rows),\n99|    }\n100|\n101|\n102|def product_barrier_profile(p: int, word: Iterable[int]) -> dict[str, object]:\n103|    \"\"\"Evaluate ChatGPT Web's exact Product Barrier sub-lemma.\"\"\"\n104|    profile = lift_profile(p, word)\n105|    if profile[\"integral\"]:\n106|        raise ValueError(\"Product Barrier is stated only for nonintegral words\")\n107|    modulus = 1 << profile[\"m\"]\n108|    product = Fraction(1)\n109|    factors = []\n110|    for row in profile[\"rotations\"]:\n111|        if row[\"word\"][0] == 0:\n112|            continue\n113|        y = Fraction(row[\"h\"] * profile[\"A\"] + modulus, profile[\"A\"])\n114|        factor = Fraction(p) + Fraction(profile[\"b\"], y)\n115|        product *= factor\n116|        factors.append({\"index\": row[\"index\"], \"y\": y, \"factor\": factor})\n117|    barrier_holds = product < modulus if profile[\"b\"] == 1 else product > modulus\n118|    return {\n119|        \"p\": p,\n120|        \"word\": profile[\"word\"],\n121|        \"m\": profile[\"m\"],\n122|        \"k\": profile[\"k\"],\n123|        \"b\": profile[\"b\"],\n124|        \"A\": profile[\"A\"],\n125|        \"M\": modulus,\n126|        \"product\": product,\n127|        \"barrier_holds\": barrier_holds,\n128|        \"factors\": tuple(factors),\n129|    }\n130|\n131|\n132|def scan_product_barrier(p: int, max_m: int) -> dict[str, object]:\n133|    if max_m < 1:\n134|        raise ValueError(\"max_m must be positive\")\n135|    seen: set[tuple[int, ...]] = set()\n136|    nonintegral = 0\n137|    counterexamples = []\n138|    by_sign = {\"+1\": 0, \"-1\": 0}\n139|    for m in range(1, max_m + 1):\n140|        for tail in range(1 << (m - 1)):\n141|            word = (1,) + tuple((tail >> j) & 1 for j in range(m - 1))\n142|            if not is_primitive(word):\n143|                continue\n144|            necklace = min(rotations(word))\n145|            if necklace in seen:\n146|                continue\n147|            seen.add(necklace)\n148|            profile = lift_profile(p, necklace)\n149|            if profile[\"integral\"]:\n150|                continue\n151|            nonintegral += 1\n152|            sign = \"+1\" if profile[\"b\"] == 1 else \"-1\"\n153|            by_sign[sign] += 1\n154|            barrier = product_barrier_profile(p, necklace)\n155|            if not barrier[\"barrier_holds\"] and len(counterexamples) < 10:\n156|                counterexamples.append(\n157|                    {\n158|                        \"word\": \"\".join(map(str, necklace)),\n159|                        \"m\": m,\n160|                        \"k\": profile[\"k\"],\n161|                        \"b\": profile[\"b\"],\n162|                        \"A\": profile[\"A\"],\n163|                        \"product\": str(barrier[\"product\"]),\n164|                        \"M\": barrier[\"M\"],\n165|                    }\n166|                )\n167|    return {\n168|        \"p\": p,\n169|        \"max_m\": max_m,\n170|        \"primitive_necklaces\": len(seen),\n171|        \"nonintegral_necklaces\": nonintegral,\n172|        \"nonintegral_by_sign\": by_sign,\n173|        \"counterexamples\": counterexamples,\n174|    }\n175|\n176|\n177|def scan_all_words(p: int, max_m: int) -> dict[str, object]:\n178|    if max_m < 1:\n179|        raise ValueError(\"max_m must be positive\")\n180|    words = prefilter = premise = integral_premise = 0\n181|    counterexamples = []\n182|    by_m = []\n183|    for m in range(1, max_m + 1):\n184|        row = {\"m\": m, \"words\": 0, \"all_q_at_least_one\": 0, \"premise_rows\": 0, \"integral_premise_rows\": 0}\n185|        for tail in range(1 << (m - 1)):\n186|            word = (1,) + tuple((tail >> j) & 1 for j in range(m - 1))\n187|            row[\"words\"] += 1\n188|            words += 1\n189|            k = sum(word)\n190|            D = (1 << m) - p**k\n191|            b = 1 if D > 0 else -1\n192|            A = abs(D)\n193|            rotated_words = rotations(word)\n194|            Bs = tuple(B_value(p, rotated) for rotated in rotated_words)\n195|            if any(B < A for B in Bs):\n196|                continue\n197|            row[\"all_q_at_least_one\"] += 1\n198|            prefilter += 1\n199|            if any(B < A * h_residue(p, b, rotated) for B, rotated in zip(Bs, rotated_words)):\n200|                continue\n(more lines below)"}}
